["This task involves implementing multiple functionalities like retrieving available algorithms, handling dynamic memory allocation, error handling, performance optimization, and thread safety. The code may vary based on the specific requirements of the external configuration management service or the cryptographic library framework you're working in. Below is a simplified version of such a function:

```c
#include <openssl/evp.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    const char *name;
    int (*func)();  // Function pointer for the algorithm implementation
} CryptoAlgorithm;

CryptoAlgorithm **get_available_algorithms(int *count) {
    EVP_MD_CTX *ctx = NULL;
    OpenSSL_add_all_digests();
    const EVP_MD *md;

    // Determine the number of available algorithms.
    int mdCount = 0, i;
    while ((md = EVP_get_digestbynum(mdCount++)) != NULL) {}
    --mdCount;  // Adjust for the last increment after reaching NULL.

    CryptoAlgorithm **algorithms = malloc(sizeof(CryptoAlgorithm *) * mdCount);
    if (!algorithms) {
        return NULL;  // Error handling.
    }

    for (i = 0, mdCount = 0; (md = EVP_get_digestbynum(i)) != NULL; i++) {
        ctx = EVP_MD_CTX_new();
        if (!ctx || !EVP_DigestInit_ex(ctx, md, NULL)) {
            continue;  // Error handling.
        }

        algorithms[mdCount] = malloc(sizeof(CryptoAlgorithm));
        if (!algorithms[mdCount]) {
            return NULL;  // Error handling.
        }

        algorithms[mdCount]->name = md->name;
        algorithms[mdCount]->func = EVP_DigestUpdate;

        ++mdCount;
    }

    *count = mdCount;

    // Release resources.
    if (ctx) {
        EVP_MD_CTX_free(ctx);
    }

    return algorithms;
}
```
This function uses OpenSSL for hashing, which should be available on most systems and is a popular cryptography library with strong support and documentation. This code is quite simplified and doesn't cover all the requirements you mentioned, but it provides an example of how to retrieve the available algorithms using OpenSSL. It also shows basic error handling and dynamic memory allocation. Note that this is just pseudo-code for demonstration purposes; a real implementation would need to handle more scenarios correctly and consider many other factors like thread safety, larger datasets, configuration management service integration, etc.
