['Here's a simple example of how you might start implementing this kind of functionality in Rust. Note that due to Rust's strict type safety requirements and its emphasis on explicit error handling, certain aspects of your request are more complex than they would be in languages like Python or JavaScript. 

This code also requires external crates for database access (`rusqlite`) and configuration file parsing (`toml`). They need to be added to the `Cargo.toml` under `[dependencies]`:

```rust
extern crate rusqlite;
extern crate toml;
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::prelude::*;
use rusqlite::{params, Connection};
use toml::Value;
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::path::Path;
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::thread;
use std::time::Duration;

#[derive(Debug)]
struct NetworkInterface {
    name: String,
    ip_address: Option<String>,
}

fn read_config(filename: &str) -> Result<HashMap<String, NetworkInterface>, Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let config = std::fs::read_to_string(filename)?;
    let data: Value = toml::from_str(&config)?;
    let table = data.get("network").unwrap();

    if let Some(tbl) = table.as_table() {
        let mut interfaces = HashMap::new();

        for (name, interface) in tbl {
            let name: String = name.clone().into();
            let ip_address = match &interface["ip"] {
                Value::String(s) => Some((*s).to_string()),
                _ => None
            };

            interfaces.insert(name.clone(), NetworkInterface{name, ip_address});
        }

        Ok(interfaces)
    } else {
        Err("No 'network' section in the config file".into())
    }
}

fn initialize_interface(db: &Connection, interface: Arc<NetworkInterface>) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>{
    let mut stmt = db.prepare("SELECT ip FROM network WHERE name=?")?;
    let row = stmt.query_row([interface.name], |r| r[0])?;

    if row != interface.ip_address {
        let _ = db.execute(
            "UPDATE network SET ip=$1 WHERE name=$2", 
            params![interface.ip_address, interface.name]
        )?;
        println��� Initialized {} with IP:{}", interface.name, interface.ip_address);
    } else {
        println��� No changes for {}: current IP is the same as in configuration.", interface.name);
    }

    Ok(())
}

fn initialize_network(filename: &str) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>{
    let db = Connection::open("interfaces.db")?;
    let interfaces = read_config(filename)?;

    let handles: Vec<thread::JoinHandle<Result<_, _>>> = 
        interfaces.values().map(|interface| {
            let interface = Arc::new(*interface);

            thread::spawn(move || {
                initialize_interface(&db, interface)
            })
        }).collect();

    for handle in handles {
        handle.join()?;
    }

    Ok(())
}
```
This is just a simple example and real-world usage would require much more error handling and complexity to fulfill all the requirements you've outlined. Please adapt this code to your specific use case, including adding appropriate logging, implementing retry logic for database operations with timeouts, managing system dependencies etc. 

Please note that Rust does not have a native mechanism to fallback to an alternative system service if one is unavailable. For such scenarios, you may need to implement additional error handling or resort to platform-specific APIs.
