["Sure, here is a basic implementation that meets all requirements and handles each element only once (with some additional complexity of managing the maximum size). 

Please note this example uses C++14 or above due to usage of auto.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <functional>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

typedef function<int(int)> CallbackFn;

void modifyVector(vector<int> &numbers, int target, CallbackFn callback, size_t maxSize) {
    // Try to find the first occurrence of the target in the vector
    auto it = find(numbers.begin(), numbers.end(), target);

    if (it != numbers.end()) {
        // If found, replace it with the result generated by invoking the callback function on the target value. 
        *it = callback(*it);
        return;
    }

    // If vector size has reached its maximum allowed size, pop off the first element before adding a new one
    if (numbers.size() >= maxSize) {
        numbers.erase(numbers.begin());
    }

    // If not found, append a new element to the end of the vector using the callback function's result. 
    numbers.push_back(callback(target));
}
```
In this example, I have used `std::find` to find an element in the vector which is O(n), but it only does so once. If we need to frequently modify and search within a large dataset, more efficient algorithms could be used (like binary search). However, it's important to consider that each operation costs CPU cycles and memory, and thus the best approach depends on specific requirements.

Also note this function doesn't take into account the cost of invoking `callback`. If you have a heavy computation in `callback` and you expect this function to be called frequently with same inputs, it would make sense to cache computed values for efficiency. 

Finally, I used `erase()` method after checking if the vector size has reached its maximum allowed size, not before insertion. This is done to avoid resizing of the vector if possible and ensure memory optimization. 

Please be aware that this implementation may behave differently depending on the specifics of your requirements and constraints. You might need a more sophisticated approach for your use case.
"]

