['Here is a simple example of how you might implement such a system in C++ using a combination of spdlog library for flexible and efficient logging, asio for high-performance networking, and Boost Asynchronous logger for asynchronous logging. This code doesn't cover all the points mentioned in your requirements due to the complexity of implementation but it gives you an idea about how one might start implementing such a system:

```cpp
#include <spdlog/spdlog.h>
#include <spdlog/async_logger.h>
#include <spdlog/sinks/basic_file_sink.h>
#include <boost/asio/steady_timer.hpp>
#include <boost/bind.hpp>
#include <iostream>

class Logger {
public:
    Logger() :
            logger(spdlog::create<spdlog::sinks::basic_file_sink_mt>("logfile", "logs/app-%Y-%m-%d.txt")),
            workGuard(asio::make_work_guard(ioService)),
            timer(ioService, boost::posix_time::seconds(1)) {
        logger->flush_on(spdlog::level::info); // flush to file every second
    }

    ~Logger() {
        ioService.stop(); // stop the io service before destroying Logger object
        thread.join(); // wait for the logging thread to finish
    }

    void startLoggingThread() {
        timer.async_wait(boost::bind(&Logger::logMetrics, this));
        thread = std::thread([this]() { ioService.run(); });
    }

private:
    void logMetrics() {
        logger->info("Metrics: {}", getMetrics()); // log metrics to file

        timer.expires_at(timer.expires_at() + boost::posix_time::seconds(1)); // schedule next logging
        timer.async_wait(boost::bind(&Logger::logMetrics, this));
    }

    std::string getMetrics() { /* implement your own logic to fetch metrics */ return ""; }

private:
    std::shared_ptr<spdlog::logger> logger; // the actual logger object
    boost::asio::io_service ioService; // asynchronous io service
    boost::asio::executor_work_guard<boost::asio::io_service::executor_type> workGuard; // ensure run() does not return when it has no more work
    boost::asio::steady_timer timer; // a timer that will be used to schedule logMetrics
    std::thread thread; // the logging thread
};
``` 
Please note, this is just one possible implementation and there are many other ways to achieve what you asked for. This code simply logs some metrics every second into a file using spdlog and Boost Asio libraries. You will need to implement various functionalities based on your requirements such as dynamic log verbosity adjustments, different buffering strategies etc. 

