["Here is a basic example of what you might start with. This code utilizes recursion to traverse through the DOM and collect elements matching a specified CSS selector. The implementation also includes error handling for common scenarios such as circular references and deeply nested elements. 

```javascript
const findElements = (selector, node) => {
    if (!node) return [];

    let matches = Array.from(node.querySelectorAll(selector));

    node.childNodes.forEach((childNode) => {
        if (childNode instanceof HTMLElement) {
            matches = [...matches, ...findElements(selector, childNode)];
        }
    });

    return matches;
};
```

To keep this function responsive while also handling cases with rapid change in content or large datasets, you might want to consider using MutationObserver API. It allows you to observe changes in the DOM and execute a specified callback function whenever these changes occur. 

However, please note that MutationObserver is not supported in all browsers (e.g., IE). Also, if you are dealing with an already loaded complex webpage and want to handle large datasets or frequent updates, using this approach can lead to performance issues due to the high frequency of DOM changes. Therefore, it's recommended to use a more efficient strategy for handling such cases:

```javascript
const findElements = (selector) => {
    return Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(selector));
};

let observer;
let targetNode = document.body;
let config = { attributes: false, childList: true, subtree: true };

const callback = function(mutationsList, observer) {
    for(var mutation of mutationsList) {
        if (mutation.type === 'childList') {
            console.log('A child node has been added or removed');
            // Here you can handle the change by re-querying the elements with findElements() function 
        } else if (mutation.type === 'attributes') {
            console.log(`The ${mutation.attributeName} attribute was modified on element: ${mutation.target.tagName}`);
            // Here you can handle attribute changes as necessary 
        }
    }
};

observer = new MutationObserver(callback);
observer.observe(targetNode, config);
