['Here is the C function `buildPathString`:
```c
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

char *buildPathString(const char **segments, int count, int maxLength) {
    int length = 0;
    int i;
    for (i = count - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
        if ((length += strlen(segments[i]) + 1) > maxLength) {
            break;
        }
    }
    ++i;

    char *path = malloc((count + i) * sizeof(*path));
    memset(path, 0, (count + i) * sizeof(*path));
    for (; i < count; ++i) {
        if ((length += strlen(segments[i]) + 1) > maxLength && length - strlen(segments[i]) <= maxLength) {
            path[strlen(path) - 2] = '\0';
            break;
        } else {
            strcat(path, segments[i]);
            if (i < count - 1) {
                strcat(path, "/");
            }
        }
    }

    return path;
}
```
Here is the JavaScript function `dynamicSelector`:
```javascript
function dynamicSelector(rootNode, selectorFunction, callback) {
    let nodes = [];

    (function traverse(node) {
      if (selectorFunction(node)) {
        nodes.push(callback ? callback(node) : node);
        for (let i = 0; i < node.children.length; ++i) {
          traverse(node.children[i]);
        }
      } else {
        for (let sibling of node.parentNode.childNodes) {
          if (sibling === node) break;
          traverse(sibling);
        }
      }
    })(rootNode);

    return nodes;
}
```
Please note that this code does not include any error checking or validation of input parameters. It is always a good practice to check if the input arguments are valid and handle exceptions accordingly. 

Also, please consider that when using `strcat` function in C, it modifies the destination string by appending the source string at its end. In JavaScript, strings are immutable, so we need to create a new string for each concatenation operation. This is why we're allocating memory for our path in C and using `+=` operator in JavaScript. 

Lastly, please note that the performance of these functions can be improved by avoiding repeated checks for maximum length in every iteration. Instead, you could check only when there are changes in the inclusion or exclusion of segments from the path string.
