["Here is a complex task that involves multiple facets of programming including system level memory management, concurrency control, error handling and hash table design. Below is a simplified version of how to solve this problem using C. Please note that implementing the entire feature in a single function might exceed your requirement. Therefore, it's best to break down tasks into separate functions as per their responsibilities.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct record {
    int value;
} Record;

typedef struct hash_table {
    pthread_mutex_t mutex;
    int size;
    long *offsets;
    int *keys;
    Record **records;
} HashTable;

HashTable* create_hash_table(int size) {
    HashTable *ht = malloc(sizeof(HashTable));
    ht->size = size;
    ht->offsets = calloc(size, sizeof(long));
    ht->keys = calloc(size, sizeof(int));
    ht->records = calloc(size, sizeof(Record*));
    pthread_mutex_init(&ht->mutex, NULL);
    return ht;
}

unsigned int hash(int key, int size) {
    // You should implement a good hash function here. This is just an example
    return key % size;
}

void insert(HashTable *ht, int key, long offset, Record *record) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&ht->mutex);

    unsigned int index = hash(key, ht->size);

    // Handle collision by linear probing
    while (ht->keys[index] != 0 && ht->keys[index] != key) {
        index = (index + 1) % ht->size;
    }

    ht->offsets[index] = offset;
    ht->keys[index] = key;
    ht->records[index] = record;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&ht->mutex);
}

Record* lookup(HashTable *ht, int key) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&ht->mutex);

    unsigned int index = hash(key, ht->size);

    while (ht->keys[index] != 0 && ht->keys[index] != key) {
        index = (index + 1) % ht->size;
    }

    Record *record = NULL;
    if(ht->keys[index] == key)
        record = ht->records[index];

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&ht->mutex);

    return record;
}
```
For the retry mechanism and data fetching from external API, you can use libraries like libcurl for network communication and handle error codes according to the rate limit documentation. 

The hash table implementation is a simple open addressing with linear probing strategy for collision resolution. You need to implement a good hashing function (like MurmurHash) that minimizes collisions in your specific data set. This can be complex and beyond the scope of this question, but you should study various methods online.

Finally, for larger datasets, consider using a more efficient memory management strategy like memory pool or arena allocator to minimize fragmentation issues. If necessary, implement dynamic resizing of the hash table when it becomes full. This will require careful balancing between performance and memory usage while minimizing overhead. Also, make sure to use pthread library for managing concurrency and ensure thread-safety.
"]

