['Below is a basic implementation of such a system using POSIX threads in C. This program does not include all the features that you've requested (for example, error checking and integration with an external data source). However, it provides a good starting point for understanding how you might implement such a system. 

The code below is just an outline and doesn't include actual implementation of real-time task updates from external data source or thread management overhead optimizations due to simplicity and readability. It shows the basic structure that you could use for managing tasks in C with POSIX threads:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <unistd.h>

typedef struct task {
    int id;
    int priority;
    int start_time;
} Task;

// Priority queue for tasks based on their urgency
Task* pq[100];  // simple array-based priority queue, adjust size according to your requirements
int qSize = 0;

pthread_t threads[10];   // Assume a maximum of 10 worker threads. Adjust according to your requirements.
int threadCount = 0;

// Function to add new task into the priority queue
void addTask(Task* task) {
    pq[qSize++] = task;
}

// Comparison function for tasks based on their urgency
int compareTasks(const void* a, const void* b) {
    Task** taskA = (Task**)a;
    Task** taskB = (Task**)b;

    if ((*taskA)->priority < (*taskB)->priority) return -1;
    if ((*taskA)->priority > (*taskB)->priority) return 1;
    return 0;
}

// Function that worker threads execute to process tasks
void* workerThread(void *arg) {
    while (qSize > 0) {
        // Get highest priority task and remove it from queue
        qsort(pq, qSize, sizeof(Task*), compareTasks);
        Task* task = pq[--qSize];

        printf("Processing task %d with priority %d\n", task->id, task->priority);

        sleep(task->start_time);  // Simulate processing time
    }

    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    Task task1 = {.id = 1, .priority = 2, .start_time = 3};
    addTask(&task1);

    Task task2 = {.id = 2, .priority = 1, .start_time = 2};
    addTask(&task2);

    // Create worker threads for each task
    while (qSize > 0) {
        if (threadCount < 10 && pthread_create(&(threads[threadCount++]), NULL, &workerThread, NULL)) {
            printf("Failed to create thread\n");
            return 1;
        }
    }

    // Join threads after they finish executing
    for (int i = 0; i < threadCount; i++) {
        if(pthread_join(threads[i], NULL)) {
            printf("Error joining thread\n");
            return 2;
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```
This program uses a priority queue to keep track of tasks and each worker thread processes the highest priority task when it is free. Note that this implementation has no error checking or handling for resource exhaustion, real-time task updates from external data source, memory usage, thread management overhead and trade offs in terms of task processing latency optimizations. 

To build a production-ready system like this you would need to implement these features which would require a lot more code than can be provided here due to their complexity. It's recommended to have a look at existing libraries or frameworks that provide these features such as OpenMP, Intel TBB etc. depending on your requirements and constraints.
']

