int totalTasks = 0, head = 0, tail = 0, totalThreads = 0;
Task *taskQueue[10000];
pthread_t threads[MAX_THREADS];

void processTask(Task* task) {
    // Placeholder for actual processing of the task. 
}

// Function executed by each thread
void* worker(void *arg) {
    while (1) {
        Task *task;
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
        if (totalTasks == 0) {
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
            sleep(1); // Don't consume CPU when there are no tasks
            continue;
        }
        task = taskQueue[head++];
        head %= 10000;
        totalTasks--;
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

        processTask(task); // Process the task
    }
}

// Function to add a new task
void addTask(int id, int priority) {
    Task *newTask = (Task*)malloc(sizeof(Task));
    newTask->id = id;
    newTask->priority = priority;
    time(&newTask->startTime); // Record the start time of task creation

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    if (totalTasks < 10000) {
        taskQueue[tail++] = newTask;
        tail %= 10000;
        totalTasks++;

        // If there are free threads, spawn a new thread.
        if(totalThreads < MAX_THREADS && head != tail) {
            pthread_create(&threads[totalThreads++], NULL, worker, NULL);
        }
    } else {
        printf("Task queue is full!\n");
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This C program creates a thread pool that dynamically schedules and processes tasks using a task queue. It uses a mutex lock to ensure thread safety when accessing the shared task queue. Please note, this is just the basic structure of such a system. Integrating with an external task management API for real-time updates on task priorities and deadlines and handling network failures would involve socket programming and possibly a JSON parser. Error logging can be done using printf statements or more sophisticated logging libraries. 

