['Here's a C code example that implements your request:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <string.h>

// Function to process network integers 
int receive_network_integers(int* array[], size_t limit, int (*read_func)(void)) {
    // Memory allocation check
    *array = malloc(limit * sizeof(int));
    if (*array == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Memory allocation failed: %s\n", strerror(errno));
        return -1;
    }

    int total_processed = 0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < limit; i++) {
        // Read integer from network using the provided read function 
        errno = 0;
        int num = read_func();

        if (num == -1 && errno != 0) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Error reading from network: %s\n", strerror(errno));
            free(*array);
            return total_processed;
        } else if (num == -1) {
            break;
        } else {
            (*array)[i] = num;
            total_processed++;
        }
    }

    printf("Total valid integers processed: %d\n", total_processed);
    return total_processed;
}

// Function to free the memory allocated for the array 
void free_array(int* array[]) {
    if (*array != NULL) {
        free(*array);
        *array = NULL;
    } else {
        fprintf(stderr, "Trying to free an uninitialized pointer.\n");
    }
}
```

In this code, the `receive_network_integers` function receives a pointer to an integer array and a limit for the number of integers that can be read. It also takes a function pointer (`read_func`) as argument, which is used to read data from the network. This function pointer should return the next integer value when invoked, or -1 on error with `errno` set appropriately.
The `receive_network_integers` function allocates memory for the array and reads integers from the network until it reaches the limit or encounters an error. It keeps track of the total number of valid integers processed and informs the user about that number. If there's a memory allocation failure, it prints an error message and returns `-1`.
The `free_array` function is used to free the allocated memory once we are done with the array. Before freeing, it first checks if the pointer isn't NULL to prevent issues in case of double frees. If it was already freed (or wasn't initialized), it also prints an error message for debugging purposes.
