['Here is a simple implementation in C using POSIX API functions to achieve this task. This solution uses filename parsing based on its extension type for versioning. It does not use any external databases or APIs but instead stores the file metadata in the filename itself. 

Please note, implementing mechanisms to avoid race conditions and handling concurrent processes is a complex topic beyond the scope of this question due to it highly depends on specific requirements and constraints. This code should be considered as starting point and may need further adjustments based on project's specific needs:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <ctype.h>

// maximum length of a filename
#define MAX_FILENAME 255

int renameFile(const char *path, const char *filename) {
    char newFilename[MAX_FILENAME + 1] = "";
    int version = 0; // initialize to zero for simplicity. In real case scenario, this will depend on your logic.

    // Parse the filename and its extension for versioning 
    const char *dot = strrchr(filename, '.');
    if(!dot || dot == filename) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Invalid Filename: %s\n", filename);
        return -1;
    }

    // Parse the version number from the filename
    int i = atoi(dot-filename);
    if (i <= 0 || i > 9) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Invalid Filename: %s\n", filename);
        return -1;
    }

    version += i;
    snprintf(newFilename, dot-filename + 1, "%.*s_v%d", (int)(dot-filename), filename, version);
    strncat(newFilename, dot, MAX_FILENAME - (dot - filename));

    // Replace spaces and special characters with underscores
    for(char *c = newFilename; *c != '\0'; ++c) {
        if(!isalnum(*c))
            *c = '_';
    }

    char oldPath[PATH_MAX];
    snprintf(oldPath, sizeof(oldPath), "%s/%s", path, filename);

    char newPath[PATH_MAX];
    snprintf(newPath, sizeof(newPath), "%s/%s", path, newFilename);

    // Ensure the directory exists
    if (mkdir(path, 0755) == -1 && errno != EEXIST) {
        perror("Error creating directory");
        return -1;
    }

    // Attempt to rename file
    if (rename(oldPath, newPath) < 0){
        perror("Rename operation failed");
        return -1;
    }

    printf("Successfully renamed %s to %s\n", filename, newFilename);

    return 0;
}
```
Please note that this is a simple version and does not include any checks or error handling for edge cases like full filesystems or unsupported characters. Also, it assumes that the path to the directory containing the file is passed as an argument to the renameFile function. For larger applications with different requirements, you might need to adjust or improve this code significantly.
']

