["Below is an example C code snippet which provides some of the functionality mentioned in the problem statement. Please note that this code does not include all necessary mechanisms like thread-safety for hash table resizing, handling race conditions during concurrent access etc as they require deep understanding and proper consideration of context and can be highly specific depending on the exact requirements of your system.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    int value;
} Record;

typedef struct {
    long *offsets;
    int *keys;
    Record **records;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
} HashTable;

HashTable table;
size_t capacity = 1024; // initial hash table capacity
size_t size = 0; // current number of entries in the hash table
size_t threshold = (capacity * 3) / 4; // resize the table when 75% is filled

void init() {
    table.offsets = malloc(capacity * sizeof(long));
    table.keys = malloc(capacity * sizeof(int));
    table.records = malloc(capacity * sizeof(Record*));
    pthread_mutex_init(&table.lock, NULL);
}

size_t hash(int key) { return (size_t)key % capacity; } // simple hash function for illustration

void insert(int key, Record *record) {
    size_t index = hash(key);
    pthread_mutex_lock(&table.lock);

    while (table.keys[index] != 0 && table.records[index] != NULL) { // handle collisions here
        if (++index == capacity) index = 0; // wrap around to the start of the array when reaching the end
    }

    table.offsets[index] = (long)(table.keys + index);
    table.keys[index] = key;
    table.records[index] = record;
    size++;

    // resize the hash table if it's more than 75% full
    if (size >= threshold) {
        capacity *= 2; // double the capacity
        long *oldOffsets = table.offsets;
        int *oldKeys = table.keys;
        Record **oldRecords = table.records;

        init(); // reinitialize with new larger capacity

        for (size_t i = 0; i < capacity / 2; ++i) {
            if (oldKeys[i] != 0 && oldRecords[i] != NULL) {
                insert(oldKeys[i], oldRecords[i]); // re-insert the entries from the old table
            }
        }

        free(oldOffsets);
        free(oldKeys);
        free(oldRecords);
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&table.lock);
}

Record *lookup(int key) {
    size_t index = hash(key);
    pthread_mutex_lock(&table.lock);
    while (table.keys[index] != key && table.records[index] != NULL) {
        if (++index == capacity) index = 0; // wrap around to the start of the array when reaching the end
    }

    Record *record = (table.keys[index] == key ? table.records[index] : NULL);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&table.lock);
    return record;
}
``` 
This code snippet provides a simple hash table with linear probing for collision resolution and dynamic resizing when the load factor exceeds 75%. The hash function is a modulo operation, which may not be suitable for all use cases due to potential key distribution issues. It also uses a single mutex for synchronization across threads, potentially limiting concurrency.
"]

["Implementing such a high-performance semaphore management system in C requires advanced knowledge of both low-level programming concepts and operating systems fundamentals. Below is an outline that would be used to create this system but each step has been quite complex to illustrate, thus it can't be fully realized with simple code blocks.

1) Defining Semaphore Structure:

```c
typedef struct {
    int identifier;
    unsigned int value;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
    pthread_cond_t condition;
} semaphore;
```
Each semaphore is represented by a unique identifier, an initial value and two POSIX thread primitives (a mutex for synchronization and a conditional variable to handle threads waiting on the semaphore).

2) Initializing Semaphore:

```c
semaphore* init_semaphore(int id, unsigned int value){
    semaphore *s = malloc(sizeof(semaphore));
    if (s == NULL) return NULL;

    s->identifier = id;
    s->value = value;
    pthread_mutex_init(&(s->lock), NULL);
    pthread_cond_init(&(s->condition), NULL);

    return s;
}
```
Initialize a new semaphore with an identifier and initial value. 

3) Deleting Semaphore:

```c
void delete_semaphore(semaphore *s){
    pthread_mutex_destroy(&(s->lock));
    pthread_cond_destroy(&(s->condition));
    free(s);
}
```
Destroy the semaphore, releasing any resources it was using. 

4) P (Proberen - Test):

```c
void wait(semaphore *s){
    pthread_mutex_lock(&(s->lock));
    while (s->value <= 0) {
        pthread_cond_wait(&(s->condition), &(s->lock)); 
    }
    s->value--;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&(s->lock)); 
}
```
Reduce the semaphore value by one, waiting if it's already zero. This operation must be atomic and should prevent other threads from modifying the semaphore while it is being decremented.

5) V (Verhogen - Increment):

```c
void signal(semaphore *s){
    pthread_mutex_lock(&(s->lock));
    s->value++;
    pthread_cond_signal(&(s->condition)); 
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&(s->lock)); 
}
```
Increment the semaphore value by one, alerting any waiting threads if it was previously zero. This operation must be atomic and should prevent other threads from modifying the semaphore while it is being incremented.

Please note that this is a basic implementation and actual system would need to handle concurrency and synchronization effectively, provide error handling and recovery mechanisms, consider memory usage optimizations, scalability considerations etc.
