['This task is complex and would require significant code to implement. Here's a simplified sketch of what such a function might look like in C using Pthread library for thread management and pqxx library for database connections. It does not include all the features you mentioned but it includes some basics like connection pool creation, reallocation on demand, and basic error handling:

```C
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pqxx/pqxx>

typedef struct {
    pqxx::connection* conn;
} PooledConnection;

PooledConnection pool[10]; // Assume a static connection pool of 10 connections
int current_index = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void initialize_pool(char* conninfo) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
        try {
            pool[i].conn = new pqxx::connection(conninfo);
        } catch (const std::exception &e) {
            fprintf(stderr, "ERROR: %s", e.what());
            exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
        }
    }
}

pqxx::connection* acquire_connection() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (current_index >= 10) {
        fprintf(stderr, "ERROR: Connection pool exhausted\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE); // Or return nullptr, depending on your error handling strategy.
    }

    pqxx::connection* conn = pool[current_index].conn;
    ++current_index;

    if (current_index >= 10) {
        current_index = 0; // Start over when all connections are used.
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    return conn;
}

void release_connection(pqxx::connection* conn) {
    (void)conn; // Do nothing for simplicity, in real implementation you might want to check if the connection is still valid and so on.
}
```
This code doesn't implement all of your requirements like dynamic resizing or error handling in a thread-safe way but it should give an idea about how one could implement these features. It also lacks any form of load balancing, state management, optimization, etc. For the full implementation you would need to study more in-depth aspects of thread synchronization, connection pooling, and database query distribution.
