['Here is an example solution using C#'s `async` and `await` keywords to perform asynchronous batch insert operations. Error handling, dynamic schema change monitoring, and versioning are not included in this example but should be added for a full implementation. This code uses SQL Server and assumes the existence of a suitable SQL connection and command objects.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Data.SqlClient;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class Record
{
    // Define your record type here...
}

public static async Task ProcessLargeDataset(List<Record> records, SqlConnection conn, SqlCommand cmd, int maxBatchSize)
{
    try
    {
        await conn.OpenAsync();

        int batchCount = 0;
        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        List<Record> currentBatch = new List<Record>();

        foreach (var record in records)
        {
            currentBatch.Add(record);
            ++batchCount;

            if (batchCount >= maxBatchSize || record == records[records.Count - 1]) // End of dataset or batch size limit reached
            {
                tasks.Add(InsertIntoDatabaseAsync(currentBatch, cmd));
                currentBatch = new List<Record>();
                batchCount = 0;
            }
        }

        await Task.WhenAll(tasks); // Wait for all batches to finish before closing the connection
    }
    catch (Exception e)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("An error occurred: " + e.Message);
    }
    finally
    {
        if (conn != null && conn.State == System.Data.ConnectionState.Open)
            await conn.CloseAsync();
    }
}

private static async Task InsertIntoDatabaseAsync(List<Record> records, SqlCommand cmd)
{
    try
    {
        foreach (var record in records)
        {
            // Prepare and execute SQL command for each record...
            await cmd.ExecuteNonQueryAsync();
        }
    }
    catch (Exception e)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("An error occurred: " + e.Message);
    }
}
```
This solution assumes that you are inserting into a single table, and that the records being inserted are all of the same type, which is the case for SQL databases. If this isn't the case, some modifications may be necessary. 

Remember to replace `Record` with your own record class definition. This code also assumes that you have already set up a suitable `SqlConnection` and `SqlCommand` object; these would typically come from your database access layer. 

Error handling is minimal in this example, but should be extended for a production-quality solution to catch and handle any exceptions that could occur during the insertion process. This could include SQL errors, network issues, or other exceptions.
