["Due to the nature of this problem, it would be impossible to provide a complete solution in one single piece of code. However, we can break down the task into some components that we can then write as separate modules or functions.

Here is an example implementation with promises for async handling and data validation checks:
```javascript
class DataStreamProcessor {
    constructor() {
        this.dataChunks = [];
        this.totalSize = 0;
    }

    processData(chunk) {
        return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
            if (!Buffer.isBuffer(chunk)) {
                reject('Invalid chunk: not a Buffer');
            } else if (chunk.length === 0) {
                reject('Invalid chunk: empty buffer');
            } else {
                this.dataChunks.push(chunk);
                this.totalSize += chunk.length;
                resolve();
            }
        });
    }

    getConcatenatedBuffer() {
        const concatenatedBuffer = Buffer.allocUnsafe(this.totalSize);

        let offset = 0;
        for (const chunk of this.dataChunks) {
            chunk.copy(concatenatedBuffer, offset);
            offset += chunk.length;
        }

        return concatenatedBuffer;
    }
}
```
This class `DataStreamProcessor` has methods to process incoming data chunks asynchronously using promises and checks the validity of each data chunk before processing. It stores all valid chunks in an array, then concatenates them when necessary.

To handle multiple streams concurrently or at different times, you would instantiate this class for each stream:
```javascript
const processor1 = new DataStreamProcessor();
processor1.processData(chunkFromNetwork1).then(() => {
    const buffer1 = processor1.getConcatenatedBuffer();
});

const processor2 = new DataStreamProcessor();
processor2.processData(chunkFromNetwork2).then(() => {
    const buffer2 = processor2.getConcatenatedBuffer();
});
```
This way, each stream will be handled in its own instance and won't interfere with each other due to JavaScript's event-driven, non-blocking I/O model.

For error handling, you could add a `catch` block to deal with any rejections from the promise:
```javascript
processor1.processData(chunkFromNetwork1)
    .then(() => {
        const buffer = processor1.getConcatenatedBuffer();
        // process buffer...
    })
    .catch((error) => {
        console.error('Error processing data:', error);
    });
```
For performance optimization, consider using a stream API like `stream.Readable` to handle large amounts of incoming data efficiently and for memory management, you can use `Buffer.allocUnsafe()` when performance is critical but ensure all allocations are properly tracked and released by calling `buffer.readUint8(0)` before releasing the buffer.
