["Here is a simplified version of how this might be approached in JavaScript using recursion for traversal and cache for optimization. 

```javascript
class DomTraverser {
    constructor() {
        // Use a map to store selector -> result mapping
        this._cache = new Map();
    }

    // Main function which handles different selector types and matching criteria
    traverse(selector, context=document) {

        if (this._cache.has(selector)) {
            return Promise.resolve(this._cache.get(selector));
        }

        const isIdSelector = selector[0] === '#';
        const isClassSelector = selector[0] === '.';

        // Handle various selectors and matching criteria
        let result;
        if (isIdSelector) {
            result = context.getElementById(selector.slice(1));
        } else if (isClassSelector) {
            result = Array.from(context.getElementsByClassName(selector.slice(1)));
        } else {
            result = Array.from(context.getElementsByTagName(selector));
        }

        // Store in cache
        this._cache.set(selector, result);

        return Promise.resolve(result);
    }
}
```

This is a basic implementation and does not handle server-side rendering (SSR) or asynchronous element creation. For SSR support, you might need to use libraries like `cheerio` in Node.js environment to provide an API similar to the DOM that works on the server-side. For handling dynamic content updates, a mutation observer or other event handling methods can be used. To prevent infinite loops caused by circular DOM references, you could implement a mechanism where you keep track of visited nodes and break the loop if you encounter them again.

