["Here's an example of how you might write such a function in JavaScript using the `axios` library for handling HTTP requests, `p-limit` for concurrency control, and `retry` for retry logic. For simplicity, this code assumes that you already have an API key from your cloud storage provider:

```javascript
const axios = require('axios');
const pLimit = require('p-limit');
const retry = require('async-retry');

class CloudStorageUploader {
    constructor(apiKey, maxConcurrency = 10) {
        this.apiKey = apiKey;
        // Control the maximum number of simultaneous uploads
        this.limiter = pLimit(maxConcurrency);
    }

    async uploadFile(filePath, fileSize) {
        const startTime = Date.now();

        try {
            await retry(async bail => {
                try {
                    // Perform the actual HTTP request to the API
                    await this.limiter(() => 
                        axios.post('https://your-cloud-storage/upload', filePath, {
                            headers: { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${this.apiKey}` }
                        })
                    );
                } catch(err) {
                    // Retry on network errors and 5xx server responses
                    if (err.response && err.response.status >= 500 || err.code === 'ECONNABORTED') {
                        console.log('Retrying upload due to error:', err);
                        bail(err);
                    } else {
                        throw err;
                    }
                }
            }, { 
                retries: 3, // Retry a maximum of 3 times
                onRetry: (err, attempt) => console.log(`Attempt ${attempt} failed with error: ${err}`),
            });

            const endTime = Date.now();

            console.log(`Uploaded file at ${filePath} in ${(endTime - startTime)} ms, size: ${fileSize} bytes`);
        } catch (error) {
            console.error('Failed to upload file', error);
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, the `uploadFile()` function takes a file path and size as input parameters. It uses these values to perform an HTTP POST request to the cloud storage API endpoint for each file to be uploaded. If the request fails due to a network error or 5xx server response (which usually indicates temporary issues), it retries up to three times before giving up.

It also logs the time taken to upload the file and its size in bytes, providing detailed metrics on upload performance and errors. It uses `p-limit` to limit the number of concurrent uploads based on a configurable `maxConcurrency` parameter.

Please note that this is just a basic example and might need additional adjustments depending on your exact requirements (e.g., different cloud storage provider, handling progress tracking, etc.). Additionally, real-time monitoring would likely involve integrating with an external logging or metrics service, which is beyond the scope of this simple function.
