['```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <assert.h>

typedef struct Node {
    int value;
    double *ptr;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;

Node* create_list(int size, double data[], int range) {
    srand(time(NULL));

    // Allocate memory for the list.
    Node *head = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if (head == NULL) exit(-1);  // error handling.

    Node *current_node = head;

    // Initialize each node's integer field to a random value within specified range.
    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        current_node->value = rand() % range;  // random number within given range.
        if(i < sizeof(data)/sizeof(double)){
          current_node->ptr = &data[i];   // Point to specific memory location within separate data structure.
        } else {
           current_node->ptr = NULL;
        }

        if (i == size - 1) break;  // break at the last node.

        // Allocate memory for next node and set it as the next node of current node.
        current_node->next = malloc(sizeof(Node));
        if (current_node->next == NULL) exit(-1);  // error handling.

        current_node = current_node->next;  // Move to the next node.
    }

    return head;
}

// Thread safety mechanism using mutex locks
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
void traverse(Node* node) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);   // Lock the access to linked list to avoid race condition.

    while (node != NULL) {
        printf("Value: %d, Pointer Value: %f\n", node->value, *(node->ptr)); 
        node = node->next;  // Move to next node.
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);   // Unlock the access for other threads.
}
```
This code implements a dynamically allocated linked list with memory safety measures and thread safety using mutex locks. The `create_list` function creates the linked list of given size, initializes each node's integer field to a random value within a specified range, and points the double pointer to specific locations in a separate data structure. If memory allocation fails at any point, it exits with an error message. 

The `traverse` function prints the values of all nodes while providing thread safety using mutex locks. It ensures that only one thread can access or modify the linked list at a time. This prevents race conditions. However, note that this is not enough for scalability in distributed systems where multiple machines are accessing and modifying shared data concurrently. To achieve that, more sophisticated synchronization mechanisms such as distributed locking protocols might be needed, which are beyond the scope of this code.
