['Below are code snippets for a simple task management system in C. The tasks are stored in a doubly linked list where each node represents a task. The tasks can be added to the list in an ordered manner based on priority. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct Task {
    int priority;
    char* description;
    struct Task *next, *prev;
} Task;

Task *head = NULL, *tail = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void addTask(int priority, char* desc) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Task* task = (Task*)malloc(sizeof(Task));
    task->priority = priority;
    task->description = malloc(strlen(desc) + 1);
    strcpy(task->description, desc);
    task->next = NULL;

    if (head == NULL || head->priority > priority) {
        task->prev = NULL;
        task->next = head;
        if(head != NULL) { 
            head->prev = task; 
        } else { 
            tail = task;
        }
        head = task;
    } else {
        Task* current = head;
        while (current->next != NULL && current->next->priority <= priority) {
            current = current->next;
        }

        task->next = current->next;
        if(task->next != NULL) { 
            task->next->prev = task; 
        } else { 
            tail = task;
        }
        task->prev = current;
        current->next = task;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void printTasks() {
    Task* current = head;
    while(current != NULL) {
        printf("Priority: %d, Description: %s\n", current->priority, current->description);
        current = current->next;
    }
}

void cleanupTasks() {
    Task* current = head;
    while(current != NULL) {
        free(current->description);
        Task* oldCurrent = current;
        current = current->next;
        free(oldCurrent);
    }
}
```
This code does not include periodic updates from an external source or error handling. For the latter, exceptions can be thrown and caught in C++ using `try`, `catch` blocks. However, C doesn't have built-in exception support. A possible approach would involve returning specific codes for errors and managing these cases manually by checking the returned code.

For a more complete solution, it may be necessary to integrate with an external data source via a library that supports multithreading or create a thread for fetching updates from an external source. This involves creating a separate function that is executed periodically by a thread, and then updating the task list in this function. 

Please note, you should always use defensive programming techniques to prevent potential memory leaks or crashes due to null pointer dereferences. In addition, it's important to ensure proper synchronization when dealing with concurrency, as was done here using a mutex. The trade-offs between these aspects (memory usage vs thread safety) are quite complex and depend on the specific use case at hand. It may be necessary to make certain design choices based on the load conditions or future feature requirements.
