["Here's a high-level overview of how you might structure your code. This solution employs boost::unordered_map for the hash table, std::vector for the dynamic resizing strategy, and C++11 threading primitives (std::mutex and std::lock_guard) for concurrency control:

```cpp
#include <boost/unordered_map.hpp>
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <thread>
#include <random>
#include <mutex>

struct record {
    int value;
};

void update(std::forward_list<std::string>& list, std::string str1, std::string str2) {
    static boost::unordered_map<int, long> hashTable;
    static std::vector<record*> records;
    static std::mutex mtx;

    // Lock the mutex to prevent data races during access.
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

    if (hashTable.size() == hashTable.bucket_count()) {
        // If the table is nearly full, resize it to accommodate new entries.
        hashTable.reserve((hashTable.size() + 1) * 2);
    }

    auto iter = list.before_begin();
    auto end = list.end();
    bool found = false;

    while (++iter != end) {
        if (*iter == str1) {
            // If the first string is found, insert the second after it and break the loop.
            list.insert_after(iter, str2);
            found = true;
            break;
        }
    }

    if (!found) {
        // If the first string was not found, append the second to the end of the list.
        list.insert_after(end, str2);
    }

    std::random_device rd;
    std::mt19937 gen(rd());
    std::uniform_int_distribution<> distr(-5000, 5000); // adjust the range as needed.

    record* rec = new record();
    int value = distr(gen);
    rec->value = value;
    records.push_back(rec);

    hashTable[value] = static_cast<long>(&rec - &records[0]); // offset calculation
}
```

Please note: This code assumes that the forward list is not huge, since resizing the list itself might be costly in terms of time complexity. For handling massive lists, you'll need to use a data structure more suitable for this scenario (like a tree-based container or a linked list with a custom allocator).

The hash table uses boost::unordered_map due to its high cache efficiency and low memory usage characteristics. However, keep in mind that the specific collision resolution strategy used by boost is not exposed for public use and may change between versions. It's important to design your update mechanism with this factor in mind.
"]

