["This is a complex task that requires a detailed understanding of Rust's async programming model as well as some lower-level networking concepts. Below is a high-level example of how such an HTTP client could be implemented, but keep in mind that this does not cover all the features mentioned above due to its complexity and length:

```rust
use hyper::{Body, Client, Request};
use url::Url;
use futures::executor::block_on;
use std::error::Error;

pub struct HttpClient {
    client: Client<hyper::client::HttpConnector>,
}

impl Default for HttpClient {
    fn default() -> Self {
        HttpClient {
            client: Client::new(),
        }
    }
}

impl HttpClient {
    pub async fn get(&self, url: Url) -> Result<hyper::Response, Box<dyn Error>> {
        let resp = self.client.get(url.clone()).await?;
        let bytes = hyper::body::to_bytes(resp.into_body()).await?;
        Ok(bytes)
    }

    pub async fn post(&self, url: Url, body: &str) -> Result<hyper::Response, Box<dyn Error>> {
        let resp = self.client.post(url.clone()).body(Body::from(body)).await?;
        let bytes = hyper::body::to_bytes(resp.into_body()).await?;
        Ok(bytes)
    }
}
```

This code creates a struct called `HttpClient` that contains an HTTP client from the hyper library. It provides two methods: one for sending GET requests, and another for POST requests. The request URL is provided as a parameter to the function, and the body of the POST request can also be provided if required. The responses are returned as Result types so that any errors that occur during the HTTP call can be handled gracefully.

Remember that this example doesn't cover all aspects of your requirements, but it should provide you with a starting point to continue building on top of it. It does make use of Rust's async/await syntax which is available in the stable release of Rust as from version 1.39.0.
