#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct {
    int value;
    double timestamp;
    char status_flag;
} record;

int* readData(size_t *len) {
    size_t capacity = 1024;  // Initial array capacity
    int *data = malloc(capacity * sizeof(*data));

    if (data == NULL) {
        perror("Failed to allocate memory");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    size_t index = 0;
    while (scanf("%d", &data[index]) == 1) {
        if (++index == capacity) {  // If we're out of space, reallocate
            void *new_ptr = realloc(data, (capacity *= 2) * sizeof(*data));

            if (new_ptr == NULL) {
                perror("Failed to reallocate memory");
                free(data);  // Free the original block before we exit.
                exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
            }

            data = new_ptr;
        }
    }

    *len = index;
    return data;
}
```

In this function, we start by allocating a small array of integers for our data. If the scanf call is successful (i.e., it reads an integer), we store that integer in our array and increment our index counter. If our array is full, we use realloc to increase its size before continuing with the next read operation.

This function dynamically increases the size of the data array as more values are read from standard input. This way, we only need to make a single allocation for all the data, which can save time and reduce memory usage compared to allocating a separate block for each value. We also check that realloc was successful before overwriting `data` with its new location, ensuring we don't lose our original pointer in case of failure.
